552734375, 0.060882568359375, -0.0150909423828125, 0.01537322998046875, 0.004848480224609375, 0.01505279541015625, -0.03167724609375, -0.00499725341796875, 0.01812744140625, -0.00604248046875, 0.0223236083984375, -0.00801849365234375, 0.0274658203125, 0.042144775390625, 0.0283966064453125, 0.0149078369140625, 0.00905609130859375, -0.022796630859375, 0.0254058837890625, 0.049713134765625, -0.035369873046875, -0.007537841796875, -0.05029296875, -0.05242919921875, 0.0021076202392578125, -0.0010242462158203125, -0.003162384033203125, -0.006969451904296875, 0.00400543212890625, -0.011871337890625, 0.01514434814453125, -0.01153564453125, -0.0166015625, -0.0175628662109375, 0.005733489990234375, 0.0035266876220703125, 0.0064849853515625, -0.0093994140625, 0.001064300537109375, -0.00978851318359375, 0.027069091796875, 0.0031757354736328125, 0.009979248046875, 0.035064697265625, -0.002498626708984375, 0.0239410400390625, -0.010772705078125, -0.0016984939575195312, 0.020751953125, -0.0030117034912109375, -0.0009503364562988281, 0.0031223297119140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This process requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, implementations based on in-house small language models (SLMs) offer advantages in cost reduction, data security, confidentiality, and interpretability.\n\n**Introduction**  \nWe explore the use of in-house SLMs for invoice categorisation, focusing on two models: a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa). These models were selected due to their strong performance on sentence-level tasks and their suitability for fine-tuning on domain-specific data, which is essential for financial applications where data privacy is critical.\n\n**Methodology**  \nWe first analyse the pre-trained embedding geometry of SBERT and DeBERTa on a proprietary financial invoice corpus. In this context, \"embedding geometry\" refers to the spatial arrangement of sentence representations in the model\u2019s high-dimensional vector space. We found that the sentence-embedding space is globally anisotropic (i.e., the distribution of embeddings is directionally dependent), but locally forms isotropic clusters (i.e., clusters are uniform in all directions). These clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity, suggesting that vendor information is a key factor in how the models represent invoice text.\n\n**Results**  \nSBERT, fine-tuned on a single GPU using a dataset of approximately 10,000 invoices from multiple clients, achieved an accuracy of 0.96 on invoice classification. This outperformed both a zero-shot large language model (LLM) and a baseline using only vendor identity. Notably, SBERT showed improved performance for smaller, challenging categories (e.g., niche expense types with fewer than 50 examples) and for new clients, as demonstrated in case studies involving recently onboarded businesses. For the generalisation task, SBERT achieved an F1 score of 0.9 with roughly 100 client-specific invoices, indicating strong performance even with limited data.\n\n**Geometric Analysis and Interpretability**  \nOur geometric analysis, which involved visualising and quantifying the clustering of embeddings, revealed that the structure of the pre-trained embedding space is associated with classification performance. Interestingly, when we provided structured input (such as tabular or explicitly segmented invoice data) that would typically aid a human reader, the SLM\u2019s performance did not improve. This suggests that the model\u2019s internal representation already captures the relevant structure, or that the model is optimised for unstructured text, highlighting a difference between human and model interpretability.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nIn summary, our findings demonstrate that in-house SLMs like SBERT and DeBERTa are effective for invoice categorisation, offering high accuracy, strong generalisation to new clients and rare categories, and enhanced data security. The embedding geometry analysis provides insight into model behaviour, and our results suggest that further structuring of input data may not always benefit SLM performance. This approach is promising for organisations seeking secure, interpretable, and cost-effective automation of financial document processing."}}